-- title:
--   (6), p. 227 — E{Y(t+h) − Y(t) | ℱₜ} = λE{∫ₜ^{t+h} U(s)ds | ℱₜ}
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $(\Omega,\mathcal F,P)$ is a probability space; $N=\{N(t),t\ge0\}$ is a process with values in a measurable space $S$; $A=\{A(t),t\ge0\}$ is a Poisson process at rate $\lambda>0$; $B\subseteq S$ is a set with $\{N(t)\in B\}\in\mathcal F$ for every $t\ge0$; $U(t)=\mathbf 1\{N(t)\in B\}$; the sample paths of $U$ are left continuous with right-hand limits w.p. 1; $U$ is jointly measurable on $\Omega\times[0,\infty)$; and lack of anticipation holds with respect to the joint history: for each $t\ge0$, $\{A(t+u)-A(t),u\ge0\}$ is independent of $\mathcal F_t$, the σ-field generated by $\{A(s),U(s);0\le s\le t\}$.
--
--   Let $\mathcal F_t$ be the σ-field generated by $\{A(s),U(s);0\le s\le t\}$. For $t\ge0$ and $h>0$ the increment $Y(t+h)-Y(t)$ is integrable and, almost surely,
--
--   $$E\{Y(t+h)-Y(t)\mid\mathcal F_t\}=\lambda\,E\Big\{\int_t^{t+h}U(s)\,ds\ \Big|\ \mathcal F_t\Big\}.$$
--
--   This is the conditional form of (3) on which Lemma 1 rests: since the σ-field of the past of $R(t)=Y(t)-\lambda tV(t)$ is contained in $\mathcal F_t$, it yields the martingale property of $R$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The conditional expectations are Mathlib's `condExp` with respect to $\mathcal F_t$, written as a supremum of the σ-fields generated by $A(s)$ and $U(s)$, $0\le s\le t$; these random variables are measurable under the hypotheses, so $\mathcal F_t$ is a sub-σ-field of $\mathcal F$. Lack of anticipation is assumed against $\mathcal F_t$ rather than against $\{U(s),0\le s\le t\}$ alone as in the printed LAA (p. 225). The page's proof of (6) uses this joint independence ("from LAA and the independent-increments property … for any set in $\mathcal F_t$"), and the printed LAA together with independent increments does not give it: with $U=1$ on $[0,1]$, $U=G$ on $(1,2]$, $U=0$ afterwards, where $G$ is the parity of $A(2)-A(1)$ masked by a fair bit computed from the arrival times in $[0,1]$, $G$ is independent of the future increments after time $1$, so the printed LAA holds, yet $E\{R(2)-R(1)\mid R(s),s\le1\}=\pm\lambda e^{-2\lambda}\ne0$ on an event of positive probability. The joint condition implies the printed one.
-- source:
--   Wolff, Poisson Arrivals See Time Averages, Oper. Res. 30 (1982), DOI 10.1287/opre.30.2.223, p. 226–227, display (6) and the sentence before it

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_WolffPASTA_Main_Setting

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Topology

namespace WolffPASTA.Main

/-- Display (6), p. 227: with `ℱₜ` the σ-field generated by `{A(s), U(s); 0 ≤ s ≤ t}`,
`E{Y(t + h) - Y(t) | ℱₜ} = λ E{∫ₜ^{t+h} U(s) ds | ℱₜ}`.
Lack of anticipation is taken against the joint history `ℱₜ` (`LAAJoint`), the independence the
page's proof of (6) uses; the printed LAA (future increments independent of the past of `U` only)
does not imply (6) or LEMMA 1 (see the Formalization Note). -/
theorem eq_6
    {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    {S : Type*} [MeasurableSpace S] (N : ℝ → Ω → S)
    (A : ℝ → Ω → ℕ) (lam : ℝ) (hlam : 0 < lam) (hA : IsPoissonProcess P A lam)
    (B : Set S) (hB : ∀ t, 0 ≤ t → MeasurableSet {ω | N t ω ∈ B})
    (hpath : PathRegular P (U N B))
    (hjoint : JointlyMeasurable (U N B))
    (hLAA : LAAJoint P A (U N B))
    (t h : ℝ) (ht : 0 ≤ t) (hh : 0 < h) :
    Integrable (fun ω => Y (U N B) A (t + h) ω - Y (U N B) A t ω) P ∧
      P[fun ω => Y (U N B) A (t + h) ω - Y (U N B) A t ω | Fhist A (U N B) t] =ᵐ[P]
        fun ω => lam * (P[fun ω' => ∫ s in t..(t + h), U N B s ω' | Fhist A (U N B) t]) ω := by sorry

end WolffPASTA.Main
